{"num":21,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear inequalities (word problem)","text":"Ken is working this summer as part of a crew on a farm. He earned \\($8\\) per hour for the first \\(10\\) hours he worked this week. Because of his performance, his crew leader raised his salary to \\($10\\) per hour for the rest of the week. Ken saves \\(90\\%\\) of his earnings from each week. What is the least number of hours he must work the rest of the week to save at least \\($270\\) for the week?","choices":["38","33","22","16"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"22","desmos":"Type <code>0.9*(80+10h) ~ 270</code> — the squiggly-equals regression solves directly for \\(h\\), giving exactly \\(22\\), the least number of additional hours needed."},
{"num":22,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratic symmetry","text":"The function \\(p\\) is defined by \\(p(x)=a\\left[(x+6)^{2}-b\\right]\\left[(x+6)^{2}-c\\right]\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are constants. In the \\(xy\\)-plane, the graph of \\(y=p(x)\\) passes through the points \\((-7,24)\\) and \\((0,899)\\). What is the value of \\(p(-12)+p(-5)\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"923","desmos":"Type <code>p(x)=a((x+6)^2-b)((x+6)^2-c)</code> with sliders on \\(a,b,c\\) and drag them until \\(p(-7)=24\\) and \\(p(0)=899\\) both hold — then read \\(p(-12)\\) and \\(p(-5)\\) off the graph. Because \\(p\\) depends only on \\((x+6)^2\\), \\(p(-12)\\) always matches \\(p(0)\\) and \\(p(-5)\\) always matches \\(p(-7)\\) no matter how the sliders are set, so the sum always reads \\(923\\)."},
{"num":23,"type":"SPR","topic":"Systems of equations","text":"\\(5y=3-ax\\)\\n\\(-x+6=10y+5x\\)\\nThe given system of equations has infinitely many solutions. If \\(a\\) is a constant, what is the value of \\(a\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"3","desmos":"Type both equations exactly as given, <code>5y=3-a*x</code> and <code>-x+6=10y+5x</code>, with a slider on \\(a\\) — drag \\(a\\) until the two lines sit exactly on top of each other everywhere (infinitely many intersection points instead of one), which happens at \\(a=3\\)."},
{"num":24,"type":"SPR","topic":"Percentages","text":"A real estate company offers a series of three webinars. \\(2{,}000\\) people attended the first webinar. \\(65\\%\\) of the people who attended the first webinar attended the second webinar, and \\(44\\%\\) of the people who attended both the first and second webinars attended the third webinar. Of those who attended the first but did not attend the second webinar, \\(31\\%\\) attended the third webinar. How many people attended both the first and third webinars but did not attend the second webinar?","choices":null,"ans":"217","desmos":"Build the chain directly as one Desmos expression: <code>(2000-0.65*2000)*0.31</code> — this takes the 2,000 first-webinar attendees, subtracts the 65% who also attended the second webinar to isolate the first-but-not-second group, then takes 31% of that group, returning \\(217\\)."},
{"num":25,"type":"MC","topic":"Percentages (tax)","text":"A business owner plans to purchase the same model of chair for each of the \\(81\\) employees. The total budget to spend on these chairs is \\($14{,}000\\), which includes a \\(7\\%\\) sales tax. Which of the following is closest to the maximum possible price per chair, before sales tax, the business owner could pay based on this budget?","choices":["$148.15","$161.53","$172.84","$184.94"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"$161.53","desmos":"Type <code>14000/1.07/81</code> directly into Desmos as a calculator expression — it divides out the 7% sales tax first and then splits the remaining pre-tax budget evenly among the 81 chairs, returning about \\(161.53\\)."},
{"num":26,"type":"MC","topic":"Arithmetic sequences","text":"In a sequence of numbers, each term is \\(10\\) greater than the preceding term. The first term is \\(15\\), and \\(f(n)\\) represents the \\(n\\)th term in the sequence. Which of the following functions models this situation?","choices":["\\(f(n)=15(10)^{(n-1)}\\)","\\(f(n)=15(10)^{n}\\)","\\(f(n)=15+10n\\)","\\(f(n)=15+(n-1)(10)\\)"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"\\(f(n)=15+(n-1)(10)\\)","desmos":"Type <code>y=15+(x-1)(10)</code> into Desmos and trace to \\(x=1,2,3\\) — the outputs \\(15,25,35\\) match the sequence exactly, confirming the explicit linear form without testing all four choices by hand."},
{"num":27,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential growth","text":"\\(P=P_0(1+r)^y\\)\\nThe population \\(P\\) of a certain city \\(y\\) years after the last census is modeled by the equation above, where \\(r\\) is a constant and \\(P_0\\) is the population when \\(y=0\\). If during this time the population of the city decreases by a fixed percent each year, which of the following must be true?","choices":["\\(r<-1\\)","\\(-1<r<0\\)","\\(0<r<1\\)","\\(r>1\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(-1<r<0\\)","desmos":"Graph <code>y=P_0(1+r)^x</code> with a slider on \\(r\\) — drag \\(r\\) between \\(-1\\) and \\(0\\) and watch the curve decay smoothly toward zero without ever going negative, which is exactly a fixed-percent decrease each year; sliding \\(r\\) below \\(-1\\) or above \\(0\\) breaks that behavior."},
{"num":28,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential functions","text":"\\(f(x)=250(5)^{x}\\)\\nThe function \\(f\\) is defined by the given equation. If \\(g(x)=f(x-2)\\), which of the following equations defines the function \\(g\\)?","choices":["\\(g(x)=10(5)^{x}\\)","\\(g(x)=125(5)^{x}\\)","\\(g(x)=250(25)^{x}\\)","\\(g(x)=250(10)^{x}\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(g(x)=10(5)^{x}\\)","desmos":"Graph <code>y=250*5^x</code> and <code>y=250*5^(x-2)</code> together — the second curve is just the first shifted 2 units right; dividing \\(250\\) by \\(5^2\\) to see the new leading coefficient of \\(10\\) confirms \\(g(x)=10(5)^x\\)."},
{"num":29,"type":"MC","topic":"Polynomial identities","text":"\\((ax+3)(5x^{2}-bx+4)=20x^{3}-9x^{2}-2x+12\\)\\nThe equation above is true for all \\(x\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants. What is the value of \\(ab\\)?","choices":["18","20","24","40"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"24","desmos":"Define <code>f(x)=(a*x+3)(5x^2-b*x+4)</code> with sliders for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), then nudge the sliders until \\(f(x)\\) visually overlays <code>20x^3-9x^2-2x+12</code> for every \\(x\\) — the matching sliders read \\(a=4\\), \\(b=6\\), giving \\(ab=24\\)."},
{"num":30,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratics (discriminant)","text":"For the given equation, \\(8rx^{2}-80sx+8r=0\\), \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are constants such that \\(r>0\\), \\(s<0\\), and \\(\\frac{r}{s}>k\\). What is the least value of \\(k\\) that ensures the equation has two distinct real solutions?","choices":null,"ans":"-5","desmos":"Graph <code>y=8r*x^2-80s*x+8r</code> with sliders for \\(r\\) (positive) and \\(s\\) (negative), then slide the ratio \\(r/s\\) and watch for the parabola to cross the x-axis twice (two real roots) versus just touching it once — the boundary case marks \\(k=-5\\)."},
{"num":31,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear inequalities","text":"A psychologist set up an experiment to study the tendency of a person to select the first item when presented with a series of items. In the experiment, \\(300\\) people were presented with a set of five pictures arranged in random order. Each person was asked to choose the most appealing picture. Of the first \\(150\\) participants, \\(36\\) chose the first picture in the set. Among the remaining \\(150\\) participants, \\(p\\) people chose the first picture in the set. If more than \\(20\\%\\) of all participants chose the first picture in the set, which of the following inequalities best describes the possible values of \\(p\\)?","choices":["\\(p>0.20(300-36)\\), where \\(p\\le150\\)","\\(p>0.20(300+36)\\), where \\(p\\le150\\)","\\(p-36>0.20(300)\\), where \\(p\\le150\\)","\\(p+36>0.20(300)\\), where \\(p\\le150\\)"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"\\(p+36>0.20(300)\\), where \\(p\\le150\\)","desmos":"Type the inequality <code>36+p>0.20*300</code> directly into Desmos and it shades the solution region on the number line, giving \\(p>24\\) — matching choice D once the \\(36\\) is isolated on the left side."},
{"num":32,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratics (Vieta's)","text":"\\((x-k)^{2}=(k-5a)(x-k)\\)\\nIn the given equation, \\(a\\) and \\(k\\) are constants, where \\(k>5a\\). The sum of the solutions to the equation is \\(3k+32\\). What is the value of \\(a\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"-6.4","desmos":"Graph <code>y=(x-k)^2-(k-5a)(x-k)</code> with sliders for \\(a\\) and \\(k\\) — the two x-intercepts are \\(x=k\\) and \\(x=2k-5a\\); add a readout for their sum \\((3k-5a)\\) and slide until it equals \\(3k+32\\), which forces \\(a=-6.4\\)."},
{"num":33,"type":"SPR","topic":"Polynomial roots","text":"\\((3x+p)(5x^{2}-45)(2x^{2}-16x+6p)=0\\)\\nIn the given equation, \\(p\\) is a positive constant. The sum of the solutions to the equation is \\(\\frac{20}{3}\\). What is the value of \\(p\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"4","desmos":"Graph <code>y=(3x+p)(5x^2-45)(2x^2-16x+6p)</code> with a slider on \\(p\\) — trace all the x-intercepts and sum them with a readout, then slide \\(p\\) until that sum equals \\(20/3\\), landing at \\(p=4\\)."},
{"num":34,"type":"MC","topic":"Unit conversion","text":"A certain park has an area of \\(11{,}863{,}808\\) square yards. What is the area, in square miles, of this park? (\\(1\\) mile \\(=1{,}760\\) yards)","choices":["1.96","3.83","3,444.39","6,740.8"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"3.83","desmos":"Type <code>11863808/1760^2</code> directly into Desmos — it converts square yards to square miles in one step, giving \\(3.83\\)."},
{"num":35,"type":"MC","topic":"Radical functions","text":"The function \\(f\\) is defined by \\(f(x)=m\\sqrt{-x-n}\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are constants. If \\(f(8)=0\\) and \\(f(4)<-16\\), which of the following must be true?","choices":["\\(m>-8\\)","\\(n+m=0\\)","\\(n+m>0\\)","\\(n-m>0\\)"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"\\(n-m>0\\)","desmos":"Define <code>f(x)=m*sqrt(-x-n)</code> with sliders for \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), then drag until \\(f(8)=0\\) (tracing the point) and \\(f(4)\\) drops below \\(-16\\) — watching which slider region satisfies both shows \\(m\\) must be less than \\(-8\\), which is exactly equivalent to \\(n-m>0\\)."},
{"num":36,"type":"SPR","topic":"Function evaluation","text":"Let the function \\(p\\) be defined as \\(p(x)=\\frac{(x-c)^{2}+160}{2c}\\), where \\(c\\) is a constant. If \\(p(c)=10\\), what is the value of \\(p(12)\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"11","desmos":"Define <code>p(x)=((x-c)^2+160)/(2c)</code> with a slider for \\(c\\), drag \\(c\\) until \\(p(c)=10\\) (trace the point at \\(x=c\\)), which lands at \\(c=8\\), then evaluate <code>p(12)</code> directly by typing it into the calculator — it reads \\(11\\)."},
{"num":37,"type":"MC","topic":"Piecewise sequences","text":"Jake has a monthly budget of \\(500\\) dollars for groceries. He plans to reduce this budget by \\(10\\%\\) each month for the next three months to save for a holiday. After these three months, he will further reduce his budget by a fixed amount of \\(30\\) dollars each month for the remaining two months of the year. Let \\(b_n\\) represent Jake's grocery budget for the \\(n\\)th month. Which equation can help him find his new budget \\(b_n\\) for any month \\(n\\) within this year, where \\(1\\le n\\le12\\)?","choices":["\\(b_n=500\\times0.90^{n}\\) for \\(1\\le n\\le3\\), \\(b_n=b_{n-1}-30\\) for \\(4\\le n\\le12\\)","\\(b_n=10\\times0.90^{n}\\) for \\(1\\le n\\le5\\), \\(b_n=b_{n-1}-30\\) for \\(5\\le n\\le12\\)","\\(b_n=500\\times0.1^{n}\\) for \\(1\\le n\\le3\\), \\(b_n=b_{n-1}-30\\) for \\(4\\le n\\le12\\)","\\(b_n=50\\times0.90^{n}\\) for \\(1\\le n\\le3\\), \\(b_n=b_{n-1}-30\\) for \\(4\\le n\\le12\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(b_n=500\\times0.90^{n}\\) for \\(1\\le n\\le3\\), \\(b_n=b_{n-1}-30\\) for \\(4\\le n\\le12\\)","desmos":"Build a table in Desmos with \\(n=1\\) to \\(12\\) and compute \\(b_n=500*0.9^n\\) for the first three rows, then \\(b_n=b_{n-1}-30\\) for the rest — reading off the generated values matches choice A exactly, confirming the 10% compounding followed by flat \\$30 drops."},
{"num":38,"type":"MC","topic":"Rational exponents","text":"Given the expression \\(\\sqrt[4]{40n}\\times\\sqrt[5]{(40n)^{3}}\\), find the value of \\(y\\) for which the expression is equivalent to \\((40n)^{30y}\\), where \\(n>1\\).","choices":["\\(\\frac{17}{600}\\)","\\(\\frac{10}{600}\\)","\\(\\frac{7}{600}\\)","\\(\\frac{14}{600}\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(\\frac{17}{600}\\)","desmos":"Type <code>(40n)^(1/4)*(40n)^(3/5) ~ (40n)^(30y)</code> into Desmos with \\(n\\) set to any number greater than \\(1\\) — the squiggly-equals regression solves directly for \\(y\\), giving \\(17/600\\)."},
{"num":39,"type":"MC","topic":"Percentages","text":"The positive number \\(a\\) is \\(2{,}184\\%\\) of the sum of the positive numbers \\(b\\) and \\(c\\), and \\(b\\) is \\(84\\%\\) of \\(c\\). What percent of \\(b\\) is \\(a\\)?","choices":["4,784","2,600","40.19","22.68"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"4,784","desmos":"Type <code>a=21.84*(0.84*c+c)</code> and <code>b=0.84*c</code> into Desmos with \\(c=1\\), then type <code>a/b*100</code> to read the percent directly — it evaluates to \\(4784\\)."},
{"num":40,"type":"MC","topic":"Quadratic modeling","text":"A chemical reaction in a closed container produces a gas, and the volume \\(V\\) of gas produced over time \\(t\\) (in minutes) can be modeled by a quadratic function. At \\(t=5.0\\) minutes, the volume of gas produced is \\(40.0\\) liters, and at \\(t=15.0\\) minutes, the volume of gas produced is \\(160.0\\) liters. If the reaction starts at \\(t=0\\) with no gas produced, what will be the volume of gas produced at \\(t=25.0\\) minutes?","choices":["375.2 liters","483.6 liters","423.3 liters","333.5 liters"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"333.5 liters","desmos":"Enter a table with \\(t\\)-values \\(0,5,15\\) and \\(V\\)-values \\(0,40,160\\), then fit <code>y_1 \\sim a*x_1^2+b*x_1+c</code> using Desmos's regression — it solves for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) (with \\(c=0\\)), and evaluating the resulting quadratic at \\(x=25\\) gives about \\(333.3\\), closest to choice D."},
{"num":41,"type":"MC","topic":"Quadratics (vertex form)","text":"In the \\(xy\\)-plane, a parabola has vertex \\((9,-14)\\) and intersects the \\(x\\)-axis at two points. If the equation of the parabola is written in the form \\(y=ax^{2}+bx+c\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are constants, which of the following could be the value of \\(a+b+c\\)?","choices":["-23","-19","-14","-12"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"-12","desmos":"Graph <code>y=a(x-9)^2-14</code> with a slider for \\(a\\) restricted to \\(a>0\\) (required for a vertex below the axis to cross it twice), then trace the value at \\(x=1\\) (that's \\(a+b+c\\)) as you drag \\(a\\) — it always stays above \\(-14\\), so only \\(-12\\) is ever reachable."},
{"num":42,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratics (Vieta's)","text":"The solutions to \\(x^{2}+12x+35=0\\) are \\(r\\) and \\(s\\), where \\(r<s\\). The solutions to \\(x^{2}+12x+27=0\\) are \\(t\\) and \\(u\\), where \\(t<u\\). The solutions to \\(x^{2}+24x+c=0\\), where \\(c\\) is a constant, are \\(r+u\\) and \\(s+t\\). What is the value of \\(c\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"140","desmos":"Type <code>x^2+12x+35=0</code> and <code>x^2+12x+27=0</code> into Desmos and read the zero crossings for \\(r,s,t,u\\) directly off the graph, then type <code>(r+u)*(s+t)</code> into the calculator using those values — it evaluates straight to \\(c=140\\)."},
{"num":43,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear equations (word problem)","text":"The table above gives the typical amounts of energy per gram, expressed in both food calories and kilojoules, of the three macronutrients in food: protein (\\(4.0\\) cal/g), fat (\\(9.0\\) cal/g), and carbohydrate (\\(4.0\\) cal/g). If the \\(180\\) food calories in a granola bar come entirely from \\(p\\) grams of protein, \\(f\\) grams of fat, and \\(c\\) grams of carbohydrate, which of the following expresses \\(f\\) in terms of \\(p\\) and \\(c\\)?","choices":["\\(f=20+\\frac{4}{9}(p+c)\\)","\\(f=20-\\frac{4}{9}(p+c)\\)","\\(f=20-\\frac{4}{9}(p-c)\\)","\\(f=20+\\frac{9}{4}(p+c)\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(f=20-\\frac{4}{9}(p+c)\\)","desmos":"Type <code>4p+9f+4c~180</code> into Desmos as an implicit equation with sliders on \\(p\\) and \\(c\\), then read \\(f\\) off the resulting curve for any \\(p,c\\) pair — it matches \\(f=20-\\frac{4}{9}(p+c)\\), confirming choice B."},
{"num":44,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear equations (profit)","text":"A certain product costs a company \\(\\$65\\) to make. The product is sold by a salesperson who earns a commission that is equal to \\(20\\%\\) of the sales price of the product. The profit the company makes for each unit is equal to the sales price minus the combined cost of making the product and the commission. If the sales price of the product is \\(\\$100\\), which of the following equations gives the number of units, \\(u\\), of the product the company sold to make a profit of \\(\\$6{,}840\\)?","choices":["\\((100(1-0.2)-65)u=6{,}840\\)","\\((100-65)(1-0.8)u=6{,}840\\)","\\(0.8(100)-65u=6{,}840\\)","\\((0.2(100)+65)u=6{,}840\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\((100(1-0.2)-65)u=6{,}840\\)","desmos":"Type <code>(100*(1-0.2)-65)*u ~ 6840</code> into Desmos — the squiggly-equals regression solves directly for \\(u\\), giving \\(u=456\\), confirming the profit equation in choice A."},
{"num":45,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential decay (percent change)","text":"\\(z(w)=(0.829)^{2w}\\)\\nThe function \\(z\\) is defined by the equation shown. The value of \\(z(w)\\) decreases by \\(p\\%\\) for each increase of \\(1\\) in the value of \\(w\\). Which of the following is closest to the value of \\(p\\)?","choices":["17.1","31.3","68.7","82.9"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"31.3","desmos":"Graph <code>y=(0.829)^(2x)</code> and trace two consecutive integer x-values — read the ratio of consecutive y-values (about \\(0.6872\\)), then type <code>1-0.6872</code> into the calculator to get the percent decrease, about \\(31.3\\%\\)."},
{"num":46,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential growth","text":"A culture of bacteria is growing at an exponential rate, as shown in the table: Day \\(1\\) has \\(2.5\\times10^{5}\\) bacteria per milliliter, Day \\(2\\) has \\(5.0\\times10^{5}\\), and Day \\(3\\) has \\(1.0\\times10^{6}\\). At this rate, on which day would the number of bacteria per milliliter reach \\(5.12\\times10^{8}\\)?","choices":["Day 5","Day 9","Day 11","Day 12"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"Day 12","desmos":"Enter the table of days and bacteria counts, then fit <code>y_1 \\sim a*b^x_1</code> with Desmos regression — it finds \\(a\\approx1.25\\times10^5\\) and \\(b=2\\) (doubling each day); extending the table or solving \\(a*b^x=5.12\\times10^8\\) shows day \\(12\\)."},
{"num":47,"type":"SPR","topic":"Factoring polynomials","text":"The expression \\(6x^{4}+17x^{2}+7\\) can be rewritten as \\((3x^{2}+a)(2x^{2}+b)\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive integers, or as \\((3x^{2}+c)(2x^{2}+d)\\), where \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) are positive nonintegers. What is the value of \\(a+c\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"8.5","desmos":"Define <code>f(x)=(3x^2+a)(2x^2+b)</code> with sliders for \\(a,b\\) and drag until \\(f(x)\\) overlays \\(6x^4+17x^2+7\\) — the integer solution lands at \\(a=7,b=1\\); repeat with decimal sliders for \\(c,d\\) to find \\(c=1.5\\), giving \\(a+c=8.5\\)."},
{"num":48,"type":"SPR","topic":"Systems of equations","text":"\\(y=5kx^{2}+2x+3\\)\\n\\(\\frac{y}{10}=-x\\)\\nThe system of equations above has exactly one solution. If \\(k\\) is a positive constant, what is the value of \\(k\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"2.4","desmos":"Graph <code>y=5k*x^2+2x+3</code> and <code>y=-10x</code> together with a slider on \\(k\\) — drag \\(k\\) until the parabola is exactly tangent to the line (one intersection point instead of two), which happens at \\(k=2.4\\)."},
{"num":49,"type":"MC","topic":"Quadratics (sum of roots)","text":"In the equation \\(43x^{2}+(43d+e)x+de=0\\), \\(d\\) and \\(e\\) are positive constants. If the sum of the solutions to the given equation is \\(k(43d+e)\\), where \\(k\\) is a constant, what is the value of \\(k\\)?","choices":["\\(\\frac{1}{45}\\)","\\(-\\frac{1}{43}\\)","\\(-43\\)","\\(45\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(-\\frac{1}{43}\\)","desmos":"Type <code>43x^2+(43d+e)x+d*e=0</code> into Desmos with sliders for \\(d\\) and \\(e\\), trace the two x-intercepts, sum them, and divide by \\((43d+e)\\) — the ratio comes out to \\(-1/43\\) regardless of the slider values, confirming \\(k=-1/43\\)."},
{"num":50,"type":"MC","topic":"Percentages","text":"A bookstore purchases novels at a cost of \\(\\$12.00\\) each and sells them at a price that is \\(350\\%\\) of the purchase cost. After a promotional event, the unsold novels are offered at a clearance price, which is \\(70\\%\\) off the selling price. The store also offers a special deal where customers can buy a bookmark for an additional \\(\\$1.50\\) with each novel. What is the clearance price of each novel, in dollars?","choices":["12.60","14.10","15.00","16.50"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"12.60","desmos":"Type <code>12*3.5*0.3</code> directly into Desmos — it computes the selling price (\\(12\\times3.5=42\\)) and then the 70%-off clearance price in one line, giving \\(12.60\\); the \\$1.50 bookmark fee never enters the calculation."},
{"num":51,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential functions","text":"\\(f(x)=5(0.92)^{3x}\\)\\nThe function \\(f\\) is defined by the given equation. The equation can be rewritten as \\(f(x)=5\\left(1-\\frac{p}{100}\\right)^{x}\\), where \\(p\\) is a constant. Which of the following is closest to the value of \\(p\\)?","choices":["8","12","22","24"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"22","desmos":"type 1-p/100~0.92^3 — the squiggly-equals regression solves directly for p, giving p≈22.13, so 22 is the closest choice."},
{"num":52,"type":"MC","topic":"Quadratics","text":"When the quadratic function \\(f\\) is graphed in the \\(xy\\)-plane, where \\(y=f(x)\\), its vertex is \\((-3,6)\\). One of the \\(x\\)-intercepts of this graph is \\(\\left(-\\frac{17}{4},0\\right)\\). What is the other \\(x\\)-intercept of the graph?","choices":["\\(\\left(-\\frac{29}{4},0\\right)\\)","\\(\\left(-\\frac{7}{4},0\\right)\\)","\\(\\left(\\frac{7}{4},0\\right)\\)","\\(\\left(\\frac{17}{4},0\\right)\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(\\left(-\\frac{7}{4},0\\right)\\)","desmos":"plot the vertex (-3,6) and the point (-17/4,0) in Desmos, then type 2*(-3)-(-17/4) into the expression list — this reflects the known intercept across the axis of symmetry x=-3 and returns the other intercept's x-value, -7/4, directly."},
{"num":53,"type":"SPR","topic":"Percentages","text":"On a plot of land, \\(52.0\\%\\) of the square footage is farmland and the remaining square footage is pasture. There are buildings on exactly \\(21.5\\%\\) of the square footage of the farmland, and there are buildings on exactly \\(14.0\\%\\) of the square footage of the pasture. If there are buildings on exactly \\(p\\%\\) of the square footage of the plot of land, what is the value of \\(p\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"17.9","desmos":"type 0.52*0.215+0.48*0.14 directly into Desmos's expression line and read the decimal result, then multiply by 100 to get p=17.9."},
{"num":54,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear equations","text":"Alan drives an average of 100 miles each week. His car can travel an average of 25 miles per gallon of gasoline. Alan would like to reduce his weekly expenditure on gasoline by $5. Assuming gasoline costs $4 per gallon, which equation can Alan use to determine how many fewer average miles, \\(m\\), he should drive each week?","choices":["\\(\\frac{25}{4}m=95\\)","\\(\\frac{25}{4}m=5\\)","\\(\\frac{4}{25}m=95\\)","\\(\\frac{4}{25}m=5\\)"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"\\(\\frac{4}{25}m=5\\)","desmos":"type 4/25*m=5 into Desmos and solve for m directly, or just check that 4 dollars/gallon times (m/25 gallons saved) equals the $5 target — only 4/25 m=5 expresses that relationship correctly."},
{"num":55,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential functions","text":"The functions \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) are defined by the given equations, where \\(x\\ge 0\\). Which of the following equations displays, as a constant or coefficient, the maximum value of the function it defines, where \\(x\\ge 0\\)?\\nI. \\(f(x)=16(1.24)^{x}+43\\)\\nII. \\(g(x)=8(0.72)^{x}\\)","choices":["I only","II only","I and II","Neither I nor II"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"II only","desmos":"graph y=16*1.24^x+43 and y=8*0.72^x for x≥0 — the first curve keeps rising forever with no max, while the second decreases from x=0, so trace to x=0 on the second curve and read y=8, exactly the coefficient shown in II, confirming only II displays its max as a constant."},
{"num":56,"type":"SPR","topic":"Linear functions","text":"\\(T(x)=\\frac{3}{4}\\left(\\frac{26}{7}x-800\\right)+15.50\\)\\nKyle invented a new temperature scale he called the Tutorllini scale. The function \\(T\\) gives the temperature, in degrees Torte, that corresponds to a temperature of \\(x\\) degrees Celsius. If a temperature increased by 3.3 degrees Torte, by how much did the temperature increase, in degrees Celsius?","choices":null,"ans":"\\(\\frac{77}{65}\\)","desmos":"type (3/4)*(26/7) into Desmos to confirm the slope is 39/14 degrees Torte per degree Celsius, then type 3.3/(39/14) directly into the expression line to read off the Celsius increase, 77/65 ≈ 1.185."},
{"num":57,"type":"SPR","topic":"Absolute value functions","text":"The function \\(f\\) is defined by \\(f(x)=\\frac{|x|}{s}\\), where \\(s<0\\). What is the sum of \\(f(15s)\\) and \\(f(3s)\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"-18","desmos":"type f(x)=abs(x)/s with a slider on s restricted to negative values, then evaluate f(15s)+f(3s) in Desmos's expression list — the sum stays -18 no matter which negative s the slider picks."},
{"num":58,"type":"SPR","topic":"Polynomial multiplication","text":"The functions \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) are given by \\(f(x)=x+4\\) and \\(g(x)=3x^{2}-rx+48\\). In function \\(g\\), \\(r\\) is a constant. If \\(f(x)\\cdot g(x)=3x^{3}+192\\), what is the value of \\(r\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"12","desmos":"type f(x)*g(x) with g(x)=3x^2-r*x+48 and a slider on r, then compare it against y=3x^3+192 — the two curves coincide exactly only when the slider reaches r=12, which is also what expanding (x+4)(3x^2-rx+48) by hand confirms."},
{"num":59,"type":"MC","topic":"Unit rates","text":"One gallon of paint will cover 220 square feet of a surface. A room has a total wall area of \\(w\\) square feet. Which equation represents the total amount of paint \\(P\\), in gallons, needed to paint the walls of the room twice?","choices":["\\(P=\\frac{w}{110}\\)","\\(P=440w\\)","\\(P=\\frac{w}{220}\\)","\\(P=220w\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(P=\\frac{w}{110}\\)","desmos":"type P=2w/220 into Desmos and let it auto-simplify, or just type 2/220 alone in the expression list to see it reduces to 1/110, confirming P=w/110."},
{"num":60,"type":"MC","topic":"Quadratics","text":"A quadratic function models the height, in feet, of an object above the ground in terms of the time, in seconds, after the object is launched off an elevated surface. The model indicates the object has an initial height of 10 feet above the ground and reaches its maximum height of 1,034 feet above the ground 8 seconds after being launched. Based on the model, what is the height, in feet, of the object above the ground 10 seconds after being launched?","choices":["234","778","970","1,014"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"970","desmos":"type h(x)=a(x-8)^2+1034 into Desmos, then type h(0)~10 as a squiggly-equals regression to solve for a, and finally evaluate h(10) in the expression list to read off 970."},
{"num":61,"type":"MC","topic":"Systems of inequalities","text":"A laundry service is buying detergent and fabric softener from its supplier. The supplier will deliver no more than 300 pounds in a shipment. Each container of detergent weighs 7.35 pounds, and each container of fabric softener weighs 6.2 pounds. The service wants to buy at least twice as many containers of detergent as containers of fabric softener. Let \\(d\\) represent the number of containers of detergent, and let \\(s\\) represent the number of containers of fabric softener, where \\(d\\) and \\(s\\) are nonnegative integers. Which of the following systems of inequalities best represents this situation?","choices":["\\(7.35d+6.2s\\le 300\\)\\n\\(d\\ge 2s\\)","\\(7.35d+6.2s\\le 300\\)\\n\\(2d\\ge s\\)","\\(14.7d+6.2s\\le 300\\)\\n\\(d\\ge 2s\\)","\\(14.7d+6.2s\\le 300\\)\\n\\(2d> s\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(7.35d+6.2s\\le 300\\)\\n\\(d\\ge 2s\\)","desmos":"type both inequalities exactly, 7.35d+6.2s<=300 and d>=2s, into Desmos as a two-variable system — it shades the feasible region in the d-s plane directly, matching only the constraints in choice A."},
{"num":62,"type":"SPR","topic":"Percentages","text":"A school district is forming a committee to discuss plans for the construction of a new high school. Of those invited to join the committee, \\(15\\%\\) are parents of students, \\(45\\%\\) are teachers from the current high school, \\(25\\%\\) are school and district administrators, and the remaining 6 individuals are students. How many more teachers were invited to join the committee than school and district administrators?","choices":null,"ans":"8","desmos":"type 6/(1-0.15-0.45-0.25) into Desmos to get the total committee size, 40, then type 0.45*40-0.25*40 to read off the difference, 8."},
{"num":63,"type":"SPR","topic":"Linear functions","text":"The function \\(f\\) is defined by \\(f(x)=-ax+b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants. In the \\(xy\\)-plane, the graph of \\(y=f(x)-12\\) has a \\(y\\)-intercept at \\(\\left(0,-\\frac{75}{7}\\right)\\). The product of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is 320. What is the value of \\(a\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"\\(\\frac{2240}{9}\\)","desmos":"type b-12~-75/7 as a squiggly-equals regression to solve for b directly (giving b=9/7), then type 320/b into the expression list to read off a."},
{"num":64,"type":"SPR","topic":"Absolute value functions","text":"The function \\(f\\) is defined by \\(f(x)=\\frac{|x|}{a}\\), where \\(a<0\\). What is the product of \\(f(17a)\\) and \\(f(8a)\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"136","desmos":"type f(x)=abs(x)/a with a slider on a restricted to negative values, then evaluate f(17a)*f(8a) in Desmos's expression list — it stays 136 for every negative a, confirming the product."},
{"num":65,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear expressions","text":"A science teacher is preparing the 5 stations of a science laboratory. Each station will have either Experiment A materials or Experiment B materials, but not both. Experiment A requires 6 teaspoons of salt, and Experiment B requires 4 teaspoons of salt. If \\(x\\) is the number of stations that will be set up for Experiment A and the remaining stations will be set up for Experiment B, which of the following expressions represents the total number of teaspoons of salt required?","choices":["\\(5x\\)","\\(10x\\)","\\(2x+20\\)","\\(10x+20\\)"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"\\(2x+20\\)","desmos":"type 6x+4(5-x) into Desmos's expression list and let it auto-simplify — it collapses to 2x+20, matching answer choice C."},
{"num":66,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratics","text":"The total distance \\(d\\), in meters, traveled by an object moving in a straight line can be modeled by a quadratic function that is defined in terms of \\(t\\), where \\(t\\) is the time in seconds. At a time of 10.0 seconds, the total distance traveled by the object is 50.0 meters, and at a time of 20.0 seconds, the total distance traveled by the object is 200.0 meters. If the object was at a distance of 0 meters when \\(t=0\\), then what is the total distance traveled, in meters, by the object after 30.0 seconds?","choices":null,"ans":"450","desmos":"type the regression y1~a*x^2+b*x+c against the table points (0,0),(10,50),(20,200) in Desmos to solve for a and b, then evaluate the resulting quadratic at x=30 to read off 450."},
{"num":67,"type":"MC","topic":"Perpendicular lines","text":"In the \\(xy\\)-plane, line \\(k\\) and line \\(\\ell\\) are perpendicular and intersect at the point \\((2,8)\\). If line \\(k\\) is defined by \\(y=mx+b\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(b\\) are constants and \\(m>1\\), which of the following points lies on line \\(\\ell\\)?","choices":["\\(\\left(3,8-\\frac{1}{m}\\right)\\)","\\(\\left(3,8+\\frac{1}{m}\\right)\\)","\\((3,8-m)\\)","\\((3,8+m)\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(\\left(3,8-\\frac{1}{m}\\right)\\)","desmos":"type y=mx+b through (2,8) and y=(-1/m)(x-2)+8 for line ℓ, with a slider on m>1 — trace line ℓ at x=3 and read the y-value, 8-1/m, which matches choice A exactly."},
{"num":68,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratics","text":"Function \\(f\\) is a quadratic function. The graph of \\(y=f(x)\\) in the \\(xy\\)-plane has a vertex at \\((9,-7)\\), contains the point \\((8,-9)\\), and has a \\(y\\)-intercept at \\((0,a)\\). The graph of \\(y=6\\cdot f(x)\\) has a \\(y\\)-intercept at \\((0,b)\\). What is the positive difference between \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"845","desmos":"type y=k(x-9)^2-7 into Desmos, then type k(8-9)^2-7~-9 as a squiggly-equals regression to solve k=-2; evaluate the function at x=0 to get a, multiply by 6 to get b, and type abs(a-6a) to read off 845."},
{"num":69,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear models","text":"A painting studio is offering a promotion where the first class is free, the second class is half off the regular price, and the remaining classes are regularly priced. If the regular price of a class is $26.40, which function \\(f\\) gives the total cost, in dollars, of \\(x\\) classes taken using this promotion, where \\(x>2\\)?","choices":["\\(f(x)=26.40(x-1)+13.20\\)","\\(f(x)=26.40(x-2)+13.20\\)","\\(f(x)=26.40(x-1)+13.20(x-2)\\)","\\(f(x)=26.40(x-2)+13.20(x-1)\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(f(x)=26.40(x-2)+13.20\\)","desmos":"type each choice as a function in Desmos and evaluate at x=3 (one free class, one half-off at 13.20, one full-price at 26.40, total 39.60) — only f(x)=26.40(x-2)+13.20 returns 39.60."},
{"num":70,"type":"SPR","topic":"Systems of equations","text":"\\(\\frac{3}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}x=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}y\\)\\n\\(\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{3}{6}=py+\\frac{9}{5}\\)\\nIn the given system of equations, \\(p\\) is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of \\(p\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"2","desmos":"type both equations exactly as given, 3/2 y-1/4 x=2/3-3/2 y and 1/6 x+3/6=p*y+9/5, with a slider on p — drag p until the two lines become parallel (never intersecting) to see which value gives no solution, confirming p=2."},
{"num":71,"type":"SPR","topic":"Linear equations","text":"The table below shows the coordinates of three points on a line in the \\(xy\\)-plane, where \\(k\\) and \\(n\\) are constants.\\n\\(x\\): 3, \\(k\\), 12\\n\\(y\\): 7, 11, \\(n\\)\\nIf the slope of the line is 2, what is the value of \\(k+n\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"30","desmos":"type a table in Desmos with points (3,7) and (12,n), then type (11-7)/(k-3)~2 to solve k=5, and (n-11)/(12-k)~2 to solve n=25 — add them in the expression list to get k+n=30."},
{"num":72,"type":"SPR","topic":"Inequalities","text":"In a set of four consecutive odd integers, where the integers are ordered from least to greatest, the first integer is represented by \\(z\\). The product of 24 and the third odd integer in the set is at most the value of 86 less than the sum of the first and fourth odd integers in the set. What is the greatest possible value of \\(z\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"-9","desmos":"type 24(z+4)<=(z+(z+6))-86 directly into Desmos as a one-variable inequality and look at the shaded solution region on the number line — the boundary lands at z=-8, so the greatest odd integer satisfying it is -9."},
{"num":73,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential growth","text":"A certain investment account offers a special interest rate for the first 4 months the account is open followed by a lower interest rate for the remainder of the time the account is open. Akeyla opened one of these accounts with an original account balance of $700.00 and did not make any other deposits or withdrawals. 4 months after Akeyla opened the account, the balance had increased by 0.6% of the original balance. 6 months after Akeyla opened the account, the balance had increased by an additional 0.1% of the balance at the end of the first 4 months. Every 2 months after the first 6 months, the balance had increased by an additional 0.1% of the balance 2 months before. Which of the following equations could represent the account balance \\(B(x)\\), in dollars, \\(x\\) months after the account was opened, where \\(x\\ge 4\\)?","choices":["\\(B(x)=704.20(1.001)^{\\frac{x-4}{2}}\\)","\\(B(x)=700.00(1.001)^{\\frac{x-4}{2}}\\)","\\(B(x)=704.20(1.001)^{2(x-4)}\\)","\\(B(x)=700.00(1.001)^{2(x-4)}\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(B(x)=704.20(1.001)^{\\frac{x-4}{2}}\\)","desmos":"type B(x)=700*1.006*1.001^((x-4)/2) into Desmos and evaluate at x=4 and x=6 — it returns 704.20 and then 704.90, matching the described growth and confirming choice A's form."},
{"num":74,"type":"SPR","topic":"Mixture problems","text":"How many liters of a 25% saline solution must be added to 3 liters of a 10% saline solution to obtain a 15% saline solution?","choices":null,"ans":"1.5","desmos":"type 0.10*3+0.25*x~0.15*(3+x) — the squiggly-equals regression solves directly for x, giving 1.5."},
{"num":75,"type":"MC","topic":"Factoring","text":"If \\(a=c+d\\), which of the following is equivalent to the expression \\(x^{2}-c^{2}-2cd-d^{2}\\)?","choices":["\\((x+a)^2\\)","\\((x-a)^2\\)","\\((x+a)(x-a)\\)","\\(x^2-ax-a^2\\)"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"\\((x+a)(x-a)\\)","desmos":"type x^2-c^2-2cd-d^2 and (x+a)(x-a) as two expressions in Desmos with a defined as c+d and sliders on c and d — the two graphs overlap exactly for every value, confirming the equivalence."},
{"num":76,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear functions","text":"For groups of 25 or more people, a museum charges \\(\\$21\\) per person for the first 10 people and \\(\\$10\\) for each additional person. Which function gives the total charge, in dollars, for a tour group with \\(n\\) people, where \\(n\\ge 25\\)?","choices":["\\(f(n)=21n\\)","\\(f(n)=10n\\)","\\(f(n)=210+10(n-10)\\)","\\(f(n)=200+21(n-10)\\)"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"\\(f(n)=210+10(n-10)\\)","desmos":"type <code>f(n)=210+10(n-10)</code> and each other choice as its own function, then evaluate all of them at n=25 with <code>f(25)</code> — only this formula gives 210 for the first 10 people plus 150 for the next 15, confirming choice C."},
{"num":77,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratics","text":"In the \\(xy\\)-plane, a line with equation \\(2y=4.5\\) intersects a parabola at exactly one point. If the parabola has equation \\(y=-4x^{2}+bx\\), where \\(b\\) is a positive constant, what is the value of \\(b\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"6","desmos":"type <code>2y=4.5</code> and <code>y=-4x^2+bx</code> with a slider on \\(b\\) — drag \\(b\\) until the line is exactly tangent to the parabola (one intersection point instead of two or zero), which happens right at \\(b=6\\)."},
{"num":78,"type":"SPR","topic":"Rational functions","text":"The function \\(f\\) is defined by \\(f(x)=\\frac{x^{2}+ax+b}{2x+c}\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are constants. The graph of the function \\(f\\) in the \\(xy\\)-plane, where \\(y=f(x)\\), does not intersect the line \\(x=4\\). If \\(f(5)=f(6)=0\\), what is the value of \\(a+b+c\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"11","desmos":"type <code>f(x)=(x^2+ax+b)/(2x+c)</code> with sliders on a, b, c, then set the constraints <code>f(5)=0</code>, <code>f(6)=0</code>, and <code>2(4)+c=0</code> (the vertical asymptote at x=4) — solving gives a=-11, b=30, c=-8, so a+b+c=11."},
{"num":79,"type":"MC","topic":"Percentages","text":"There are \\(b\\) bricks that need to be stacked. After \\(k\\) of them have been stacked, then in terms of \\(b\\) and \\(k\\), what percent of the bricks have not yet been stacked?","choices":["\\(\\dfrac{b}{100(b-k)}\\%\\)","\\(\\dfrac{100(b-k)}{b}\\%\\)","\\(\\dfrac{100b}{k}\\%\\)","\\(\\dfrac{100k}{b}\\%\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(\\dfrac{100(b-k)}{b}\\%\\)","desmos":"type <code>100(b-k)/b</code> into Desmos with sample slider values (e.g. b=100, k=30) and check it reads 70 — the fraction of unstacked bricks times 100, confirming the structure of choice B."},
{"num":80,"type":"MC","topic":"Ratios and proportions","text":"A total of 454 gardeners selected at random were asked if they preferred perennials or annuals. The results of the survey are shown in the table below.\\nAnnuals: 159\\nPerennials: 295\\nAccording to these results, if 5,448 gardeners were choosing plants, how many more would prefer perennials than would prefer annuals?","choices":["136","1,632","1,908","3,540"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"1,632","desmos":"type <code>5448/454*295 - 5448/454*159</code> directly into Desmos's expression line — it scales both survey counts by the same factor (5448/454 = 12) and subtracts, giving 1,632 immediately."},
{"num":81,"type":"SPR","topic":"Linear inequalities","text":"\\(I=\\dfrac{V}{R}\\)\\nThe formula above is Ohm's law for an electric circuit with current \\(I\\), in amperes, potential difference \\(V\\), in volts, and resistance \\(R\\), in ohms. A circuit has a resistance of 500 ohms, and its potential difference will be generated by \\(n\\) six-volt batteries that produce a total potential difference of \\(6n\\) volts. If the circuit is to have a current of no more than 0.25 ampere, what is the greatest number, \\(n\\), of six-volt batteries that can be used?","choices":null,"ans":"20","desmos":"type <code>6n/500 \\le 0.25</code> into Desmos and drag an n-slider upward until the inequality just stops holding — the boundary is at n=20.8333, so the greatest whole number of batteries is 20."},
{"num":82,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear inequalities","text":"When several resistors are connected in series in a circuit, the total resistance of the circuit is the sum of the resistances of these resistors. A certain circuit consists of 8 resistors connected in series, where the resistance of each resistor is positive. This circuit has a total resistance of 130 ohms. The total resistance of 3 resistors in this circuit is 90 ohms. Which inequality best represents all possible values of the resistance \\(z\\), in ohms, of one of the other 5 resistors in this circuit?","choices":["\\(0<z<8\\)","\\(0<z<40\\)","\\(40<z<90\\)","\\(40<z<130\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(0<z<40\\)","desmos":"type <code>0<z</code> and <code>z<130-90</code> into Desmos as a one-variable inequality — the other 5 resistors must sum to 40 ohms with each one positive, so any single one of them, z, satisfies 0<z<40, matching choice B."},
{"num":83,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear functions","text":"Oil and gas production in a certain area dropped from 4 million barrels in 2000 to 1.9 million barrels in 2013. Assuming that the oil and gas production decreased at a constant rate, which of the following linear functions \\(f\\) best models the production, in millions of barrels, \\(t\\) years after the year 2000?","choices":["\\(f(t)=\\dfrac{21}{130}t+4\\)","\\(f(t)=\\dfrac{19}{130}t+4\\)","\\(f(t)=-\\dfrac{21}{130}t+4\\)","\\(f(t)=-\\dfrac{19}{130}t+4\\)"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"\\(f(t)=-\\dfrac{21}{130}t+4\\)","desmos":"enter the two data points as a table, (0,4) and (13,1.9), then fit <code>y_1 \\sim mx_1+b</code> with linear regression — Desmos returns slope m = -21/130 and intercept b = 4, matching choice C exactly."},
{"num":84,"type":"MC","topic":"Quadratics","text":"\\(f(x)=ax^{2}+6x+c\\)\\nIn the given quadratic function, \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) are constants. The graph of \\(y=f(x)\\) in the \\(xy\\)-plane is a parabola that opens upward and has a vertex at the point \\((h,k)\\), where \\(h\\) and \\(k\\) are constants. If \\(k>0\\) and \\(f(-7)=f(5)\\), which of the following must be true?\\nI. \\(c>3\\)\\nII. \\(a\\le 3\\)","choices":["I only","II only","I and II","Neither I nor II"],"ansIdx":2,"ans":"I and II","desmos":"type <code>f(x)=ax^2+6x+c</code> with sliders on a and c, then drag a until the axis of symmetry (the midpoint of x=-7 and x=5, which is x=-1) lines up — this forces a=3, and raising c from 0 shows the vertex height k stays positive only once c>3, confirming both I and II."},
{"num":85,"type":"SPR","topic":"Quadratics","text":"The function \\(h\\) is defined by \\(h(x)=ax^{2}+b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive constants. The graph of \\(y=h(x)\\) in the \\(xy\\)-plane passes through the points \\((0,10)\\) and \\((-2,36)\\). What is the value of \\(ab\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"65","desmos":"type <code>h(x)=ax^2+b</code> into Desmos with sliders on a and b, plot the points (0,10) and (-2,36), and adjust the sliders until the curve passes through both — it settles at b=10 and a=6.5, so ab=65."},
{"num":86,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear functions","text":"For groups of 25 or more people, a museum charges \\(\\$21\\) per person for the first 25 people and \\(\\$14\\) for each additional person. Which function \\(f\\) gives the total charge, in dollars, for a tour group with \\(n\\) people, where \\(n\\ge 25\\)?","choices":["\\(f(n)=14n+175\\)","\\(f(n)=14n+525\\)","\\(f(n)=35n-350\\)","\\(f(n)=14n+21\\)"],"ansIdx":0,"ans":"\\(f(n)=14n+175\\)","desmos":"type <code>f(n)=14n+175</code> plus each other choice as separate functions, then evaluate all four at n=25 — only f(n)=14n+175 returns 525, the correct charge for exactly 25 people at $21 each, confirming choice A."},
{"num":87,"type":"SPR","topic":"Exponents","text":"In the given equation, \\(p>1\\) and \\(t>1\\).\\n\\(p^{\\frac{4}{7}}=t^{\\frac{5}{4}}\\)\\nIf \\(t=p^{2n-1}\\), where \\(n\\) is a constant, what is the value of \\(n\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"\\(\\dfrac{51}{70}\\)","desmos":"type <code>p^(4/7)=t^(5/4)</code> and <code>t=p^(2n-1)</code> into Desmos with sliders on p, t, and n (holding p>1), then adjust n until both equations hold simultaneously for every p you try — the slider settles at n=51/70≈0.7286."},
{"num":88,"type":"SPR","topic":"Percentages","text":"The number \\(a\\) is 70% less than the positive number \\(b\\). The number \\(c\\) is 80% greater than \\(a\\). The number \\(c\\) is how many times \\(b\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"0.54","desmos":"type <code>a=b-0.7b</code>, then <code>c=a+0.8a</code>, then <code>c/b</code> into Desmos with any slider value for b (e.g. b=1) — it evaluates directly to 0.54."},
{"num":89,"type":"MC","topic":"Scale and ratios","text":"A square map has a side length of 35 inches, and 1 inch on the map represents an actual distance of 17 miles. A smaller version of the same map is printed as a square with the side length 55% shorter than the side length of the previous map. On the smaller map, which of the following is closest to the actual distance, in miles, represented by 1 inch?","choices":["7.65","10.97","19.25","37.78"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"37.78","desmos":"type <code>35*17/(35*0.45)</code> directly into Desmos's expression line — it divides the total actual distance spanned by the original map (35×17 miles) by the new, 45%-sized side length (35×0.45 inches), giving about 37.78 miles per inch."},
{"num":90,"type":"MC","topic":"Quadratics","text":"If \\(p(x)=x^{2}+kx+4\\) has no real zeros, what is the range of possible values for \\(k\\)?","choices":["\\(k<-4\\)","\\(-4<k<4\\)","\\(k>4\\)","No range, \\(k\\) can be any real number"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(-4<k<4\\)","desmos":"type <code>y=x^2+kx+4</code> into Desmos with a slider on k and watch the graph — the parabola stays entirely above the x-axis (no real zeros) only while k is dragged strictly between -4 and 4."},
{"num":91,"type":"MC","topic":"Systems of equations","text":"\\(\\begin{cases}ax+y=c\\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3}y=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{cases}\\)\\nIn the system of equations above, \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) are constants. If the system of equations has an infinite number of solutions \\((x,y)\\), what is the value of \\(a\\)?","choices":["\\(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)","0","\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)","\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\)","desmos":"type both equations exactly, <code>ax+y=c</code> and <code>x/2+y/3=1/6</code>, with sliders on a and c — drag a until the first line sits exactly on top of the second line for whatever c you try, which happens right at a=3/2."},
{"num":92,"type":"MC","topic":"Exponential functions","text":"\\(f(x)=3^{-2(x+1)}\\)\\nWhich of the following equivalent forms of the given function \\(f\\) displays, as the base or coefficient, the \\(y\\)-coordinate of the \\(y\\)-intercept of the graph of \\(y=f(x)\\) in the \\(xy\\)-plane?","choices":["\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{3^{2x+2}}\\)","\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2x}\\)","\\(f(x)=81\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2x}\\)","\\(f(x)=3^{-2x-2}\\)"],"ansIdx":1,"ans":"\\(f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2x}\\)","desmos":"type <code>f(x)=3^(-2(x+1))</code> into Desmos and check <code>f(0)</code> — it evaluates to 1/9, and only the choice written with 1/9 sitting out front as a visible coefficient (B) displays that y-intercept directly."},
{"num":93,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear inequalities","text":"Adam's school is a 20-minute walk or a 5-minute bus ride away from his house. The bus runs once every 30 minutes, and the number of minutes, \\(w\\), that Adam waits for the bus varies between 0 and 30. Which of the following inequalities gives the values of \\(w\\) for which it would be faster for Adam to walk to school?","choices":["\\(w-5<20\\)","\\(w-5>20\\)","\\(w+5<20\\)","\\(w+5>20\\)"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"\\(w+5>20\\)","desmos":"type <code>w+5>20</code> into Desmos as a one-variable inequality and read the shaded region on the number line (w>15) — walking (a fixed 20 minutes) beats the bus exactly when the wait plus the 5-minute ride exceeds 20, matching choice D."},
{"num":94,"type":"SPR","topic":"Polynomials","text":"\\(34z^{14}+bz^{7}+70\\)\\nIn the given expression, \\(b\\) is a positive integer. If \\(qz^{7}+r\\) is a factor of the expression, where \\(q\\) and \\(r\\) are positive integers, what is the greatest possible value of \\(b\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"2381","desmos":"substitute \\(u=z^7\\) and type <code>34u^2+bu+70</code> into Desmos, then try factor pairs of 34 (like 34×1) and of 70 (like 1×70) as (qu+r)(mu+n) — pairing the largest divisors across from each other maximizes the cross terms, giving b = 34·70 + 1·1 = 2381."},
{"num":95,"type":"MC","topic":"Linear equations","text":"The equation \\(9x+5=a(x+b)\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants, has no solutions. Which of the following must be true?\\nI. \\(a=9\\)\\nII. \\(b=5\\)\\nIII. \\(b\\ne \\dfrac{5}{9}\\)","choices":["None","I only","II and III only","I and III only"],"ansIdx":3,"ans":"I and III only","desmos":"type <code>9x+5=a(x+b)</code>, rearranged as <code>9x+5=ax+ab</code>, with sliders on a and b — the two sides become parallel, non-overlapping lines (no solution) only when a is dragged to exactly 9 and b is kept away from 5/9, confirming I and III."},
{"num":96,"type":"SPR","topic":"Linear inequalities","text":"A local transit company sells a monthly pass for \\(\\$95\\) that allows an unlimited number of trips of any length. Tickets for individual trips cost \\(\\$1.50\\), \\(\\$2.50\\), or \\(\\$3.50\\), depending on the length of the trip. What is the minimum number of trips per month for which a monthly pass could cost less than purchasing individual tickets for trips?","choices":null,"ans":"28","desmos":"type <code>95<3.5x</code> into Desmos as a one-variable inequality (using the priciest $3.50 ticket, the best case for the pass to win) and read the boundary — it solves to x>27.14, so the minimum whole number of trips is 28."},
{"num":97,"type":"SPR","topic":"Polynomials","text":"One of the factors of \\(2x^{3}+42x^{2}+208x\\) is \\(x+b\\), where \\(b\\) is a positive constant. What is the smallest possible value of \\(b\\)?","choices":null,"ans":"8","desmos":"type <code>2x^3+42x^2+208x</code> into Desmos and use its zeros/roots feature to find the x-intercepts — they land at x=0, x=-8, and x=-13, so the factors are x, (x+8), and (x+13), making the smallest possible b equal to 8."}
